  "variation": {
    "seeded": true,
    "notes": "Spike placement, sizes, lean, the growth per row, debris paths and the fissure pattern vary per cast. The gather (0.6 s), the hang, the slam time, the row rhythm and the 100 degree arc stay fixed so the hit always lands at the same moment and the danger area is fair to read."
  },
  "director_notes": "Built from Lisa's request for an earth ground pound that grows outward from the caster in a frontal arc, throwing earth up with increasing effect; it is a genre move, so nothing here copies a particular game. Timing is the point: (1) a 0.6 s gather with lifting pebbles, inward dust, a fist glow and a low rumble, then an 80 ms hang where everything holds still, so the slam lands; (2) the slam is short and hard: impact plus boom, dust pop, chunks thrown, crater, the first big jolt; (3) an eruption layer bursts rock spikes up in five rows across a 100 degree arc, each row about 1.4x bigger and 10% later than the last (row_delay_ms 130, delay_growth 1.1), so the beat builds instead of staying even; a linear arc ring of dust (ring arc_deg, ease linear) and fissures that spread outward (decal reveal_ms) run with the front; (4) the last row is the climax: the biggest spikes, a second, deeper boom, heavy debris and a dust plume at the target, and the strongest shake; (5) the rocks hold, then crumble back down; the fissures and crater outlive the spell. Sound: rumble -> whoosh -> impact + boom -> crash every 140 ms over a rumble -> deep boom + crash -> quiet rumble. A game can swap spikes for slabs (eruption shape slabs) for a heaving-ground look. The slam is at the caster's feet; a game with a jump-slam animation should trigger the slam beat on the landing frame."
}
